// ======================================================================
// Build stamp. Shown on the sign-in screen and in the footer so you can
// tell at a glance whether the deployed site is the version you pushed —
// GitHub Pages and the browser cache can both lag a commit behind.
//
// `build` matches the ?v= cache-busting number on every asset URL in
// index.html; bump both together when releasing.
//
// TUNO uses the SAME two-series idea as ENCA (see its js/version.js for the
// full rationale): production builds are plain integers on `main`; beta
// builds live in their own series that can never collide, and `cycle` IS NOT
// DERIVED FROM `build` — set it once per cycle, when the first beta build
// after a production release is cut.
//
// ONE DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE FROM ENCA. Its "five digits NNNII" rule only
// works because ENCA's production numbers are in the hundreds — TUNO starts
// at build 1, where cycle 2 iteration 1 would be 00201 = 201, which is NOT
// >= 10000 and would read as a production build. TUNO therefore anchors the
// beta series explicitly:  beta build = 10000 + cycle*100 + iteration.
// Cycle 2, iteration 1 → 10201 → renders v1.0.2-beta.1. isBeta stays the
// same >= 10000 test, and the two series stay disjoint at any cycle number.
// ======================================================================
const APP_BUILD = {
  version: "1.0",
  cycle: 3,          // hand-set cycle name — first beta cycle after production 2
  build: 10449,      // beta series: 10000 + cycle*100 + iteration → v1.0.3-beta.149
  date: "2026-08-26",
  // When this build was cut, UTC — set with `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ`,
  // never by hand (a local time typed into a UTC field puts the sign-in
  // stamp an hour into the future; ENCA builds 25090-25092 proved it).
  released: "2026-08-26T11:16Z",
  get isBeta() { return this.build >= 10000; },
  get stamp() { return `${this.label} · ${this.releasedLocal}`; },
  get releasedLocal() {
    const raw = String(this.released || "");
    const d = new Date(raw);
    if (isNaN(d.getTime())) return raw.replace("T", " ");
    try {
      const parts = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, {
        year: "numeric", month: "2-digit", day: "2-digit",
        hour: "2-digit", minute: "2-digit", hourCycle: "h23", timeZoneName: "short",
      }).formatToParts(d);
      const g = (t) => (parts.find((x) => x.type === t) || {}).value || "";
      if (!g("year")) return raw.replace("T", " ");
      const zone = g("timeZoneName");
      return `${g("year")}-${g("month")}-${g("day")} ${g("hour")}:${g("minute")}${zone ? ` ${zone}` : ""}`;
    } catch { return raw.replace("T", " "); }
  },
  get releasedUtc() { return String(this.released || "").replace("T", " ").replace("Z", " UTC"); },
  get timeZone() { try { return Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone || ""; } catch { return ""; } },
  // beta build = 10000 + cycle*100 + iteration (see the header) — so the
  // iteration is what remains above the anchored cycle base, unbounded past 99.
  get iteration() {
    return this.isBeta ? this.build - 10000 - this.cycle * 100 : this.build % 100;
  },
  get label() {
    return this.isBeta
      ? `v${this.version}.${this.cycle}-beta.${this.iteration}`
      : `v${this.version}.${this.build}`;
  },
  get full() { return `${this.label} · ${this.date}`; },
};

// Per-tool versions, keyed by the tile id in index.html. Same discipline as
// ENCA: each tool moves at its own pace; bump the tool you touched.
//
// `t` is THE TOOL'S PERMANENT NUMBER — assigned in the order the tool entered
// TUNO, NEVER reused, and a new tool takes the next free number in the same
// commit that adds the tile. ❓ Help, 📋 What's new and 🗺 Roadmap are
// deliberately not numbered: they are the app describing itself.
//
// A tool that has reached PRODUCTION is at least 1.0; it is the BETA chip,
// not the version number, that says "still proving itself".
const TOOL_VERSIONS = {
  toolLaps: { t: 18, v: "0.1", note: "BETA — R29, after Ugur Koc's Get Windows LAPS Audit (MIT): which Windows devices have an escrowed local administrator password and how old it is — NOT ESCROWED and STALE are the two shapes of one finding, a LAPS policy that is not applying, found before the day somebody needs the password that is not there. THE READS: directory/deviceLocalCredentials (beta, metadata only — id, deviceName, lastBackupDateTime) cross-referenced against Intune Windows devices on the ENTRA DEVICE ID, the original's join key. PASSWORDS ARE NEVER RETRIEVED AND THERE IS NO SWITCH THAT WOULD: the scope is DeviceLocalCredential.ReadBasic.All — the FIRST NEW PERMISSION since Device.Read.All at 10330, taken in the open per the R18 rule, and ReadBasic deliberately because Graph cannot return a password value through it; the full Read.All stays with R10's helpdesk viewer if that ever ships. TWO GATES NAMED BEFORE THE RUN: the scope at the click, and Graph's own DIRECTORY-ROLE gate on the endpoint (Intune Administrator among the allowed) — a 403 after consent is detected and explained as 'who you are, not what TUNO may do', never a bare status code. FIVE BUCKETS, not the original's four: healthy / stale (threshold yours, default 60 days) / not escrowed / AGE UNKNOWN (escrowed, unparseable backup time — neither healthy nor stale, because either would be a guess) / UNMATCHABLE (no Entra device id on the enrolment record — the original counts these not-escrowed, which accuses a machine it never looked up). Escrow records no enrolled device claims (retired machines, passwords still held) are their own list, never silently ignored. Cards double as bucket filters (T09), rows fold on device ids (T03), click-through to the 🖥 Device analyzer. Reads only. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolMaa: { t: 17, v: "0.1", note: "BETA — R27, after Ugur Koc's MAA Compliance Dashboard Report (MIT): whether multi-admin approval is protecting anything, which nothing in the portal shows. THE READS: operationApprovalPolicies and operationApprovalRequests (both beta, both under the RBAC read), the four gated inventories (apps, scripts, settings catalog, roles) as context, roleAssignments + transitive group members for the admins, approver groups transitive for who can approve — request status tolerated as string OR number because tenants answer both ways, the original's own hedge. COVERAGE IS COMPUTED THE WAY MAA WORKS: a policy gates its operation TYPE tenant-wide, so one app policy protects every app; a category with no policy has NO GATE, which is the finding; device-action types are listed as action gates, not inventories; an inventory that could not be read is UNKNOWN, never zero, and the gate verdict stands on the policy list. THE FINDING THE ORIGINAL CANNOT MAKE: a policy whose approver groups hold no members is A GATE NOBODY CAN OPEN — every operation it gates sits pending until someone is added; checked transitively, and a group that could not be read makes the count unknown, not zero. THE ADMINS TABLE CARRIES T07's SENTENCE verbatim on screen and in every export: Intune RBAC only — Global Administrator and Intune Administrator do not appear in deviceManagement/roleAssignments, so the accounts with the most access are not in the admins-without-MAA count. 'No MAA policies' is an answer, not an error, and reads as one. Approved requests without parseable timestamps sit OUTSIDE the approval-time numbers with their count stated. FEWER SCOPES THAN THE ORIGINAL: it asks six including Directory.Read.All; everything here rides already-consented reads, names via getByIds (R07's technique). Reads only, NO NEW SCOPE. Exports MD + requests CSV + admins CSV" },
  toolEndpointSec: { t: 16, v: "0.1", note: "BETA — R26, after Ugur Koc's Get Firewall and ASR Status (MIT): whether the Windows fleet actually has firewall and attack-surface-reduction enforcement, OR JUST POLICY OBJECTS. Every endpoint security policy — settings catalog policies with an endpointSecurity template family, FILTERED CLIENT-SIDE because the server-side filter is unreliable on this surface (his note, kept) — plus the legacy security intents, grouped into the portal's own disciplines with a verdict each: covered / policies exist but none reaches anybody / no policy at all. 'COVERED' IS NARROWED FROM THE ORIGINAL: assigned AND reaching somebody BY CONSTRUCTION, the T12 rule — no include and no tenant-wide target is nobody, exclusions-only is nobody, an assignment filter caps reach at may; per-device applicability is not evaluated and the screen says so, and empty included groups are T09's finding, pointed at rather than half-repeated. LEGACY INTENTS ARE CLASSIFIED, NOT DUMPED — the original lists intents under a 'Legacy Intent' pseudo-discipline so a tenant enforcing firewall the old way reads as a firewall GAP, an overstatement in the dangerous direction; here the intent's template resolves it into its discipline (one read of deviceManagement/templates), an unclassifiable one counts toward nothing and says so, and the legacy surface's assigned-or-not-only answer is a caveat on the row. THE DEVICE COUNT IS THE DENOMINATOR: a firewall or ASR gap is that many machines on local defaults, and a count that could not be read is unknown, not zero. Group names via resolveNames (getByIds); unresolved names are stated, never silently GUIDs. Discipline cards double as filters, rows fold to their assignments (open set on policy ids, the T03 rule). Reads only, NO NEW SCOPE. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolDefender: { t: 15, v: "0.2", note: "v0.2 (build 10446) adds THE DEVICE SEARCH: a box in the static toolbar — typing survives the list re-rendering under it — that appears only AFTER a fleet has been read, because its suggestions come from the fleet and an autocomplete with nothing behind it is a promise nothing keeps. Suggestions are ranked (name-starts beats name-contains beats user/id), keyboard-navigable, and picking one filters the list to that device AND opens its fold — the point of finding a machine is reading it. Free typing is itself a live filter over name, user and exact id, combinable with the bucket cards; an empty query suggests NOTHING, because an autocomplete that lists the fleet unprompted is a dump, not a search. No Graph call per keystroke — the fleet is already in memory. Original: BETA — R25, after Ugur Koc's Get Defender Status Report (MIT): the machines where Defender is silently off or outdated, before they become incident tickets. TWO READS, BOTH BETA because that is where Graph keeps protection state: deviceProtectionOverview for the tenant rollup, then every Windows device's windowsProtectionState — real-time protection, tamper protection, malware protection, signature currency, overdue quick and full scans, pending reboot, a deviceState that is not clean — each device carrying its LIST of findings rather than one traffic light. THE PER-DEVICE READ IS THE WHOLE COST and the original pays it one device at a time with a sleep; folded into $batch here, twenty a trip, the R12/R19 technique. THREE HONESTY RULES: a device that never reported protection state is ITS OWN BUCKET, never healthy by omission — a machine Defender has never reported on is exactly the machine this report exists to find, and a state that could not be READ lands in the same bucket with its error stated (unknown is not clean, the T09 rule); DISABLED AND NOT-REPORTED ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS — the original counts a null flag as off, here a flag the device did not say reads 'not reported', per flag, on the row and in both exports; and the tenant rollup CAN DISAGREE with the per-device sums because Graph refreshes it on its own schedule — both shown, labelled, never reconciled. Cards double as bucket filters (the T09 pattern), rows fold open to the full state (open set keyed on device ids, the T03 rule), a device click lands in the 🖥 Device analyzer through the tile's own handler. Reads only, NO NEW SCOPE — both endpoints under the device read T06 brought in. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolFilters: { t: 14, v: "0.1", note: "BETA — R21, after Alper Atar's IntuneShade (MIT): the filters themselves, at last. READ: every assignment filter with platform, rule and management type; per-filter USAGE as a separate optional read through GroupUse's OWN source table (the T06 rule — a surface added there is counted here), tenant-wide targets included because they carry filters too; the used-by column is ABSENT, NOT ZERO, until the scan runs, and an unread surface makes every zero a floor, said on screen. WRITE — the third thing in TUNO that writes, T11's rules sized for one object: write scope at the click; edit preceded by a FRESH READ with a drift stop keyed on lastModifiedDateTime (changed since load = REFUSED, decide against the current rule); every write verified by read-back field-for-field; delete confirmed by TYPING the name. THE GATE THE TOOL EXISTS FOR: delete refused while usage is non-zero, verified against the tenant's payload associations AT THE MOMENT OF THE ACT (one $expand=payloads read), never against the cached scan — and 'could not check' refuses too, because a filter deleted while in use silently widens every assignment that carried it to the whole target group. After a write the cached usage scan is dropped (absent again), not trusted stale. First PATCH and first DELETE in the application — added to the Graph layer under the existing no-retry write rule. Platform is fixed after creation (Graph's rule, surfaced in the form). Exports MD" },
  toolCompliance: { t: 13, v: "0.3", note: "BETA — R22 + R28. v0.3 (build 10428) adds the COVERAGE SECTION, after Ugur Koc's Get Compliance Policy Coverage (MIT): the platforms nothing evaluates — enrolled devices with no compliance policy that reaches anybody — computed ENTIRELY from the two reads the report already does (the R19 rule, zero new Graph traffic, no new scope). Platform identity is the script's own 1:1 OData-type mapping; device OSes normalise to T05's one vocabulary. COVERED IS THE R26 CLAIM: assigned AND reaching somebody by construction — exclusions-only and unassigned cover nothing, a filter caps the verdict at may (worn on the row), and group scoping WITHIN a platform is not evaluated, said on screen. A PLATFORM IS THREE ANSWERS: gap (devices, no covering policy), covered, and no-devices — context, not a gap, so an unassigned macOS policy in a Windows-only tenant does not read as a hole. THE ORIGINAL'S FOOTNOTE BECOMES A READ: deviceManagement/settings secureByDefault says whether unassigned devices are marked Compliant — the lean that decides whether a gap silently passes Conditional Access — stated next to the table, and an unreadable setting is said with the portal path, never guessed. Either half of the read failing makes coverage UNKNOWN, NOT UNCOVERED. Coverage CSV export joins the other three. — v0.2 (build 10415) applies the 10413 layout from the four-tool mockup round: the estate strip becomes FIVE STAT CARDS with percentages (compliant, non-compliant, in grace, stale with the also-count-compliant tension on the card itself, unknown-other), and the policy table becomes FOLDED ROWS that unfold in place to the full rollup, the failing settings worst-first, and the status gaps — the open set keyed on policy ids so a re-render keeps cards open, the T03 rule. Verdict accents: clean green, failing red, gap amber. Everything read is unchanged. Original: after Alper Atar's IntuneShade (MIT): the estate-wide compliance rollup TUNO answered only per-device (T06). One read: managedDevices for the estate (compliant / non-compliant / in grace / unknown, configManager named under other rather than dropped), deviceCompliancePolicies with assignments, then TWO CHEAP ROLLUPS PER POLICY folded into $batch — deviceStatusOverview and deviceSettingStateSummaries, with only the settings actually failing somewhere shown, worst first. STALE IS ITS OWN COLUMN, NOT A FLAVOUR OF COMPLIANT: threshold on screen (default 30 days, Intune's own status-validity default), a device that never synced counts stale, and a compliant-but-stale machine sits in BOTH columns with the tension stated — resolving it quietly in either direction would be a lie in that direction. The numbers are Graph's cheap rollup refreshed on Intune's schedule and labelled so; per-device drill-down is named as T06's job; a policy whose statuses could not be read is LISTED with a status-gap tag (unknown is not clean), an unassigned policy is a finding tag, and a failed estate or policy read marks that half unknown rather than zero. No new scope — devices came with T06, config with T04. Reads only. Exports MD, CSV, stale-devices CSV" },
  toolConflict: { t: 12, v: "0.2", note: "BETA — v0.2 (build 10418) applies the 10413 layout, last of the four: stat cards for the verdicts (can collide, may, cannot, settings set by more than one policy, surfaces unread when it applies), and each conflict is a FOLDED row — closed, the setting, the verdict badge and the policy count; open, the side-by-side grid the finding exists for, every policy with its value and its reach tags. Open set keyed on section|setting, surviving re-renders. Every verdict caveat, the redaction rule and the unread-surfaces honesty unchanged. Original:  R20, after Alper Atar's IntuneShade (MIT): the same setting configured to different values in two policies, found by IDENTITY, never display name — definition id in the settings catalog, definition+category in ADMX, typed property WITHIN one policy type for device configurations; a cross-surface collision on the same CSP is stated as NOT DETECTED rather than guessed, because matching a legacy typed property to a catalog definition would be display-name matching wearing a canonical hat (a departure from the card's first draft, said on the card). THE READ IS T05's collect() — same surfaces, same redaction, same failure honesty; a second copy of thirteen-surface reading is how two tools start disagreeing about one tenant. One step past the original: a collision is only a conflict IF THE POLICIES CAN MEET ON A DEVICE — verdicts computed from assignments: can collide (shared included group, or tenant-wide meeting reach), may collide (different groups CAN share members; an assignment filter caps any verdict at may — the filter id now rides on Docs.assignmentOf for exactly this), cannot collide ONLY where one side reaches nobody by construction. Agreement is not a finding; redacted values are never compared (two secrets are not known to be equal); unread surfaces are named as unknown, not clean. Findings, not a score. Reads only. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolAssignEdit: { t: 11, v: "0.5", note: "BETA — v0.4 (build 10404, Toolkit parity): TENANT-WIDE TARGETS and ASSIGNMENT FILTERS join the bar. The target seg offers Group / All devices / All users — Graph's only two tenant-wide targets; the plan header for one reads THE WHOLE TENANT, now and every device or user it gains later, and add-exclude on a tenant-wide target is REFUSED because Graph has no tenant-wide exclusion (an exclusion names a group). A filter dropdown (fed by T14's own Filters.list — same scope, one request, a tenant without filters keeps No filter) puts an assignment filter with include/exclude mode on ADDS only: a removal takes the whole assignment, filter included, and removing-just-the-filter would be an edit wearing a removal's name. The groups read scope now rides the read-policies consent, so the group box suggests from the tenant from the first keystroke. v0.3 (build 10400): the operation moves to a FLOATING SELECTION BAR at the bottom — ENCA's selbar, floated — that exists exactly while something is ticked: count, operation seg, group input and dry run travel with the selection, a ✕ clears it and the bar goes with it, and the list keeps its full height. Chosen over a sticky top bar in a mockup round. The bar lives inside the tool's own section so leaving the tool hides it, and its left edge tracks the content column's formula in both sidebar states. v0.2 (build 10390): the Intune Toolkit's own shape at last — SURFACES ON THE LEFT (a sticky rail with per-surface counts and tick counts, All on top, unreadable surfaces named under it), FILTER ON TOP, the list beside. And the fix that made the rail honest: THE SELECTION LIVES IN JS, NOT THE DOM — it used to be read off the checkboxes, so every filter keystroke re-rendered the table and silently dropped every tick, and a surface rail would have made cross-surface picking impossible. Checkboxes render FROM the set and write INTO it, picked() never looks at the DOM, ticking never re-renders the table (scroll position survives bulk ticking), the rail's ✓ counts follow live, and a fresh read clears the set because a fresh read is a fresh decision. — bulk assignment editing: add an include, add an exclusion, or remove a group across many policies at once, the thing the portal makes you do one blade at a time. After Maxime Guillemin's Intune-Toolkit (MIT), whose surface map and question this is; THE WRITE DISCIPLINE IS TUNO'S OWN, because this is the second thing in TUNO that writes and the first that writes AT SCALE — the mistake it enables is not a duplicate policy but a policy reaching the wrong population, invisible until somebody's laptop behaves differently. FOUR SURFACES, ALL UNDER THE ONE WRITE SCOPE THE REGISTRATION ALREADY DECLARES: device configurations, settings catalog, compliance, administrative templates. Scripts and applications are deliberately absent — each would be a NEW write scope, and adding one is a decision to take in the open (the R18 rule), not a feature's side effect. THE PIPELINE IS THE TOOL: read → plan → backup → confirm → apply → verify, enforced by the screen. The dry run lists EVERY operation with the group's NAME and MEMBER COUNT (an empty group is warned as configuring nothing), noops are listed as noops, and two operations are REFUSED outright with T09's reasoning: an include onto a policy that excludes the group would create the include+exclude contradiction, and an exclusion onto a policy that includes it is a contradiction, not a removal — remove the include instead. The backup (every affected policy's current assignments, as a restorable file) is taken automatically and applying stays locked until it is; removals are confirmed separately from additions, by TYPING the group's name. /assign REPLACES the whole list, so untouched assignments are re-serialised to exactly the fields the API takes — FILTERS INCLUDED, because dropping a filter on an untouched assignment would silently widen it. Every write is preceded by a FRESH read: a policy whose assignments changed since the plan was made is skipped as DRIFTED rather than overwritten, because replacing a list somebody else just edited is how two admins erase each other. Every write is followed by a read-back, and 'verified' is the tenant's word, not the request's status code. Writes never retry; sequential, never pooled; stop-on-first-failure on by default. NO DELETE, NO RENAME — the Toolkit has both behind its own Advanced Actions toggle and neither belongs in a browser tool whose whole argument is that it cannot do much harm. The write scope is asked for at the apply click and never before. After Maxime Guillemin's Intune Toolkit (MIT)" },
  toolSetSearch: { t: 10, v: "0.1", note: "BETA — 'which policies configure BitLocker, and to what value?', answered from the setting DEFINITIONS rather than the policy names. The last piece of Ugur Koc's IntuneAssignmentChecker (MIT) worth having. T05 searches the settings of policies it has already read, which finds a setting only if you already know which policy holds it; this searches the other way round, against the settings-catalog definition catalog — roughly seventeen thousand definitions, which are Microsoft's and not the tenant's. TWO READS, DELIBERATELY SEPARATE: the catalog once per session, held in memory and searched as you type with ranked token matching (name-starts beats name-contains beats keyword beats description, every token must land somewhere, and an empty query is no results rather than seventeen thousand); then, optionally, the tenant's own settings-catalog policies with their settings — the same N+1 the backup pays, pooled — so each result can say WHO sets it and TO WHAT. UNTIL THAT SECOND READ RUNS THE USAGE COLUMN IS ABSENT, NOT EMPTY, and the screen and the export both say so, because 'nobody sets this' and 'nobody looked' are different answers. Child settings are indexed under their own definition ids, so a definition configured as somebody's choice-child is still found. VALUES PASS THE DOCUMENTER'S REDACTION GATE — Docs.redactValue, the same implementation, not a copy — because a settings search is exactly the tool somebody uses to hunt for 'password', and a result table printing secret values would be a disclosure engine with a search box. A policy whose settings could not be read is named, and its absence from the usage column is stated rather than silent. Reads only, one scope. After Ugur Koc's Intune Assignment Checker (MIT)" },
  toolHealth: { t: 9, v: "0.2", note: "BETA — v0.2 (build 10416) applies the 10413 layout: SIX STAT CARDS, one per finding kind, that double as FILTERS — click a kind to see only it, click again for everything, with the failing card saying NOT CHECKED rather than a reassuring zero when status was off; the five stacked tables become ONE flat list of FOLDED findings, badge saying the kind, unfolding in place to the evidence — which group, which id, what the finding means and what it does not — with the open set keyed on stable finding keys so it survives both re-renders and the kind filter. Original:  the assignments that exist and reach nobody, in one place, every finding the same shape of problem: something that looks configured and is not. After Ugur Koc's IntuneAssignmentChecker (MIT), whose idea this is — an assignment reaching an empty group is a FINDING, not a row. SIX FINDING KINDS: assignments into EMPTY groups, checked transitively with a one-row peek rather than a count because 'is there at least one member' is the whole question and a $top=1 page batches where the text/plain count endpoint does not; DANGLING references, distinct from empty because a dangling id names a group the directory no longer has while an empty group still exists and simply holds nobody — different fault, same symptom, both listed; UNASSIGNED policies, which T05 can filter for and this treats as a finding, with enrolment restrictions deliberately exempt because their built-in defaults are unassigned by design and flagging them would bury the real rows; EXCLUDED-ONLY policies, where every assignment is an exclusion and there is no include to carve from; one group both INCLUDED AND EXCLUDED on the same policy, where the exclusion wins and the include reads as reach it does not deliver; and optionally FAILED DEPLOYMENTS, from the cheap per-policy status Graph keeps for device configurations, compliance policies, scripts and remediations. THE STATUS COVERAGE IS PARTIAL AND SAYS SO: settings catalog, ADMX, apps and app protection keep their status behind the reports API and are named as UNCHECKED rather than passed off as healthy, because a health page that silently checks only some surfaces reads as a clean bill for the rest. FINDINGS, NOT A SCORE — whether an empty group is a mistake or a landing zone prepared for next quarter is not something this tool can know. A resolveNames failure is UNKNOWN, never 'all dangling'; an unpeekable group is unknown, not empty; a failed surface is unknown, not clean. The unassigned detection rides GroupUse.intuneHits as an opt-in flag, keyed on the assignments array being empty rather than on no hits matching, so T02/T06/T08 are unchanged. Assignment reads via GroupUse.SOURCES. Reads only. After Ugur Koc's Intune Assignment Checker (MIT)" },
  toolWhatIf: { t: 8, v: "0.1", note: "BETA — what would change if this user or device joined, or left, that group: the question asked while a change is still cheap to reverse, and the one capability of Ugur Koc's IntuneAssignmentChecker that TUNO had nothing like. THE ENDPOINT LIST IS NOT A SECOND COPY: assignments are read through GroupUse.SOURCES, T06's rule, because the first time a surface is added to one list and not the other this tool starts reporting 'nothing changes' about a workload it never looked at. HOW THE SIMULATION WORKS is on the screen rather than hidden, because an oracle gets trusted past its competence: memberships today are read transitively (users from /users, devices from the ENTRA device object — the Intune record cannot say which groups a machine is in), the hypothetical set is built — JOINING adds the group AND everything it is transitively a member of, because membership of a child is membership of its ancestors; LEAVING severs the direct edge and rebuilds the closure from what remains, because a parent reachable through another group is NOT lost — and every surface is read ONCE with the sweep's null match set, each policy's effect computed under both sets. FOUR DELTA STATES rather than two: gained, lost (including included-to-excluded, because JOINING A GROUP CAN TAKE POLICY AWAY — the most surprising row this tool produces), pre-excluded (no change today, but the subject cannot receive the policy later while the exclusion stands), unchanged. An app whose intent is uninstall and starts to apply says WILL BE UNINSTALLED rather than gained. A membership held only through nesting cannot be resigned from and the answer says so instead of simulating an impossible action; a dynamic group cannot be joined by hand and the answer is labelled conditional on the rule. A SIMULATION, NOT A PROMISE: filtered assignments stay 'may' in both directions, tenant-wide targets are not in the delta at all because they apply whatever the groups say, and a leave whose closure could not be fully expanded is called a FLOOR rather than an answer. Compare mode puts two to four groups side by side — each column the group plus its own parents — and shows only where they differ. Reads only; no write scope is reachable from the file. After Ugur Koc's Intune Assignment Checker (MIT)" },
  toolAppLocker: { t: 1, v: "0.20", note: "0.20: five corrections from one real working session, all in the merged card. The header is STICKY — the long card always says whose card you are scrolled into. Microsoft app coverage folded in as a section (its old card is empty; the Add-allow-rule fix rides along). The events finding CARRIES the audited-and-blocked list it promises — expandable in place, aggregated per file with counts and users, instead of a below that pointed at nothing. ONE FIX FLOW EVERYWHERE: the fleet-gap and unsigned-hash fixes open the same confirm card the rule editors use, showing the exact rule before Apply commits it — two different fix behaviours on one screen read as one of them broken. Every applied fix announces itself: ✔ Applied in the sticky header with Undo beside it, fading after a few seconds; and the publisher editor refuses an unchanged Apply with the reason (setting no upper bound used to rewrite the rule identically and read as a fix that did nothing). Plus: the unsigned-in-writable-locations finding gained its own fix — one hash rule per collection, every hash inside, the expiry warning written into the rule.;0.19: RULES AND FINDINGS ARE ONE CARD (mockup round, option A). A finding that is ABOUT a rule renders nested under that rule inside the per-collection rule tables — severity, source mark, reason, condition when it adds something, recommendation and the fix button, with the judgement editors opening in place; findings about nothing in particular (missing collections, enforcement judgements, scan directory verdicts without a rule, fleet gaps) keep the table shape at the top. The compact narrow-column summary still covers every finding; the rules stay visible in the narrow column where the table folds. One scroll instead of two over one story: here is a rule, and here is what is wrong with it. The old Rules card is empty; the sev filter chips and the whole fix framework carry over untouched — a nested fix is the same fix in a different seat.;0.18: ONE TABLE. The fleet gaps became FINDINGS — 📡-marked rows in the findings table beside the audit's and the scan's, closed through the same fix framework (publisher/hash/exact-path per the event's own evidence, mutate/undo, one click) — and the evidence card shrank to a summary: facts, one counts line naming gaps/by-design/covered/undecided/DLL-set-aside with a link into Findings, the gap report, the tenant-pull chooser, warnings. Two tables were saying the same kind of thing about the same files, which was the overview being long; covered and by-design need no rows on screen — they are counts here and sections in the gap report. The 10436 filter chips retired with the second table.;0.17: DLL events are SET ASIDE, not undecided. Generated policies omit the DLL collection deliberately — absence is the only state that restricts nothing — so every DLL load in the fleet log is the record of that decision, not a question. They leave the default view and the undecided count, get their own chip (N DLL — set aside) that brings them back with the why written in the row, take no fix button, and the gap report carries them as their own section. A draft that CARRIES DLL rules classifies them normally, because then somebody chose to police DLL loads on purpose.;0.16: the evidence chips became FILTERS (T09's cards-as-filters): click 4 gaps to close and the table shows exactly those four — the 50-row cap applies after the filter, the active chip reads as pressed, clicking it again drops the filter, and the gap report deliberately ignores it because a change-ticket document covers everything. A ✕ Clear on the card drops just the events evidence (the policy stays); a 🧹 Start over in the step-2 toolbar empties the whole table — policy, scan, evidence, tenant-pull state, manual loop marks — behind a confirm whose one promise is that nothing in the tenant is touched. The upload button now names all three files it takes: scan bundle · policy XML · events results.;0.15: events-first is a first-class entrance. The mid-loop return carries ONLY the events bundle — the policy lives in the TENANT — and the old flow answered that moment with an alert telling people to hunt for a file. The evidence card now lives OUTSIDE the policy-only body, renders the moment a bundle lands (no alert), and leads with the actual question: what to judge it against. Two answers on the card: upload the draft (same button), or PULL THE DEPLOYED APPLOCKER PROFILE FROM THE TENANT — list the custom profiles, keep only those carrying AppLocker OMA-URIs, click one and the policy is rebuilt from its RuleCollection values. Pulling adopts the profile NAME and GROUPING into the Intune form, so a later export edits the same profile in place and the deploy panel refuses to create a twin beside it, which is exactly right. Values Graph withholds are refused with a reason, never guessed.;0.14: the loop strip takes manual marks — a small ☐ on every station the session cannot verify (the portal edit above all). Three honesty rules: EVIDENCE BEATS THE MARK both ways (an auto-lit station ignores it, and Gaps with open gaps stays amber and offers no toggle at all), a hand-marked station renders DASHED with marked-by-you so a claim never dresses as evidence, and marks persist per browser because the portal edit done yesterday is still done after a refresh. The collapsed summary shows a starred tick (✓*) for hand-marks.;0.13: the audit loop strip — the page reads 1→5 top to bottom but the work is a circle, and now the circle is drawn. Seven stations above step 1 (Scan → Build → Deploy audit → Collect → Gaps → Update profile → Enforce), each lit from what is actually loaded or known to be in the tenant THIS session, never from belief: the scan bundle lights Scan with the device name, rules on the table light Build, a created-or-found audit profile lights Deploy, an uploaded events bundle lights Collect, the Gaps station carries the live open-gap count (amber while open, green at zero — same classification pass as the chips and the gap report, so the numbers cannot disagree), and the Enforce station reads the existing gate. You-are-here is the first station not done, which is also the answer to what-next; clicking a station scrolls to its part of the page. The Update station is the honest one: a portal edit is invisible to a browser tab, and the strip says so instead of pretending. Collapses to a one-line summary, remembered per browser. All colors are theme variables — light, dark and auto for free. Mockup round: live strip chosen over a Help-only walkthrough.;0.12: the fleet evidence card grows teeth — a GAP REPORT and fixes that close what it finds. Every denied file in an uploaded events bundle is classified against the draft on screen: GAP (would still be blocked, machine space — needs a decision before enforcing), BLOCKED BY DESIGN (user-writable origin — the policy working), COVERED (the draft already allows it — the block belonged to the old policy), or UNDECIDED (no rules for the type). The chips count all rows, not just the 50 shown, and ⭳ Gap report downloads the same judgement as Markdown for the change ticket — summary table, per-section detail, suggested fix per gap, the collector's own blind spots included. Each gap row carries a 🔧 fix that adds the rule to the draft through the normal mutate/undo path: PUBLISHER when the event carries a signer, HASH from the event's FileHash when not (0x-normalised; SourceFileLength 0 and the rule's description says why, and that hash rules go stale), exact PATH as the last resort with instructions to replace it — never a directory, because a directory allow from event evidence rebuilds the hole. By-design rows get the same mechanics behind a cooler label (Allow anyway) — closing one is a business decision and the button reads like one. The event-verdict matcher also learned hashes: events carry a FileHash where coverage artifacts do not, so a hash-rule fix flips its row to covered instead of reading as a gap forever.;0.11: the AppControl events-collection set joins the toolkit — Get-TunoAppControlEvents.ps1 (the evidence pump: CodeIntegrity + all four AppLocker logs into per-ID CSV/XML, an HTML report, and a tuno.applocker.events JSON bundle, all landing in the IME Logs folder named .log so Collect diagnostics gathers them), Detect-TunoAppControlEvents.ps1 (always exits 1 on purpose — the remediation IS the harvest), and Compress-TunoAppControlReport.ps1 (zips report + bundle for MDE Live Response getfile). Downloadable in step 1, deployable from the browser as the THIRD Remediation pair, and — the point — the bundle is importable: upload AppControlEvents_Bundle_*.log and every blocked or audited fleet event is matched against the draft on screen with the same matcher the coverage table uses, aggregated per file with a recommendation each: covered (the block belonged to the old policy), stays-blocked-by-design (user-writable origin — the policy working), or missing-rule (signed, machine space — add a publisher rule). Replaces the CloudFlows Remedate_ACB set with three defects fixed: MSI/Script IDs 8005/8007 were never collected so an enforced MSI block left no evidence, a global SilentlyContinue made every catch dead code, and the 48-ID CodeIntegrity query exceeded the ~23-comparison XPath limit and silently returned nothing.;0.10: the IT-TOOLS house-folder provisioning ships as a detect-and-remediate pair — Detect-TunoItToolsFolders.ps1 (new, 1.0.0) beside Initialize-TunoItToolsFolders.ps1 (1.1.0, which now PROVES SYSTEM can write by appending a provisioning record to IT-TOOLS\\LOGS, where the house scripts log), downloadable in step 1 and deployable from the browser as a second Remediation next to the AppLocker cleanup pair. Detection reports missing folders, writes open to anyone outside SYSTEM/Administrators, and SYSTEM itself unable to write — evaluated with the same write mask and trusted set the remediation verifies with, so the pair cannot loop. Unlike the cleanup pair this one is a STANDING assignment: leave it scheduled, it is the guard on the ACL the standing allow rules depend on.;0.9: BETA — AppLocker policy builder & validator, now an end-to-end workflow rather than an XML reader: scan → upload → review → export → deploy. (1) SCAN. The tool hands out Invoke-TunoAppLockerScan.ps1, served from the site so the copy downloaded always matches the build. It is a modern reimplementation of Microsoft AaronLocker's approach — native DACL evaluation instead of Sysinternals AccessChk, Deny ACEs honoured, JSON instead of Excel COM, and it runs on PowerShell 5.1 AND 7 where AaronLocker hard-fails on anything but 5.1. It finds every directory a non-administrator can write to, inventories the executables in them with signer/version/hash, reads the AppLocker event logs, and builds a publisher-first rule set with every writable directory injected as an exception. (2) UPLOAD. The same import button takes the scan's JSON bundle or a plain policy XML; the bundle carries the generated Audit and Enforce policies plus the device's own effective policy, and any of the three can be put on the table. (3) REVIEW. The static check set is joined by scan-derived findings marked 🛰 — a writable directory an allow rule still reaches, unsigned binaries that can only ever get hash rules, executions the endpoint has already refused — reached with the SAME evaluator as the Microsoft coverage table by probing a hypothetical executable dropped into the directory. (4) EXPORT. The code panel gained a second tab: the Intune custom profile (windows10CustomConfiguration, one OMA-URI per collection, the DLL collection OMITTED), built from the same serialiser as the XML. NotConfigured is never written into a generated policy: Microsoft's own guidance is that a rule collection in that state CARRYING RULES is enforced, so shipping DLL rules that way would have enforced DLL control rather than parking it. Absence is the only inert state, and the audit reports a NotConfigured collection with rules as High \u2014 it is blocking today while reading as inactive. (5) DEPLOY, from the browser. Step 5 creates the profile in the tenant you are signed in to, under rules narrower than Graph would allow: the write scope is asked for on the click and never at sign-in; a read runs first and a profile with the same name or the same AppLocker grouping STOPS the deploy rather than being overwritten; assignment is a second, separate act that names the pilot group and its member count before it does anything; and the Enforce profile stays locked until the audit profile exists in that tenant AND an uploaded scan shows nothing blocked and nothing that would have been. Convert-TunoAppLockerToIntune.ps1 remains for the command line, standalone with no customer-connection harness, offline JSON or -Online via Connect-MgGraph. Still runs entirely in the browser; nothing is uploaded. ENFORCEMENT IS NEVER AUTOMATIC: the only mode change the tool makes on its own is the one that blocks nothing. AuditOnly to Enabled opens a judgement panel listing what should be true first (time in audit across a month-end, a patch cycle and a new starter; an event log with nothing unrecognised left; a coverage table reading allowed; a tested way back) and the admin picks the mode; adding an absent collection lands in AuditOnly. The DLL panel states what enforcing DLL costs, matching the scanner and the export. The publisher check no longer fires its breadth heuristics on rules naming BOTH a publisher and a product \u2014 the shape the coverage fixes build \u2014 so the tool stopped flagging the rule it had just recommended. After Spencer Alessi's AppLockerInspector and Microsoft's AaronLocker" },
  toolDocs: { t: 5, v: "0.4", note: "BETA \u2014 thirteen configuration surfaces read once, then browsed and written down. TWO VIEWS OVER ONE READ, the T03 shape: BROWSE is the tool you use on a Tuesday \u2014 every policy in the tenant in one filterable list, settings expanded underneath, assignments resolved to group names \u2014 and DOCUMENT is the artefact an auditor asks for and nobody has. They are one tool because reading thirteen surfaces twice would be absurd: you browse what you are about to document. Settings catalog, device configurations, administrative templates, compliance, app protection, app configuration, scripts and remediations, Windows update profiles, enrolment, Autopilot, applications, assignment filters and scope tags. Exports Markdown for a wiki, a standalone HTML page that prints cleanly, and a Word document for the auditor who will edit it \u2014 with real Word tables rather than a run of paragraphs somebody has to reformat. FILTERS APPLY TO THE EXPORTS, and every export header states how many of how many objects it holds, so a filtered document cannot be mistaken for a complete one. REDACTION IS NOT A SETTING: a tenant document is the most widely circulated thing this tool produces, and a script body, certificate, pre-shared key or encrypted OMA-URI value in one is a disclosure that cannot be recalled \u2014 every such field is replaced with a marker recording that a value exists without carrying it, and there is deliberately no switch. A section that could not be read is named as unreadable rather than silently absent; a policy whose settings could not be read is still LISTED, with the gap stated, because unlike a backup a document that admits what it does not know is more useful than one missing the object entirely. Coverage after Ugur Koc's IntuneDocumentation, which is licensed Elastic 2.0 \u2014 reimplemented from its published coverage list, not forked, and sharing none of its code. That project also has a server tier its browser hands a token to; TUNO has nowhere to put one, so the collection runs in the tab, which is the more literal version of the claim. Reads only. v0.2: clicking a policy opens a POPOUT rather than expanding it in place \u2014 a settings-catalog policy has twenty-odd rows, so opening one in the list pushed everything else off the screen and closing it lost your place; ENCA's modal, same classes. And policies are SELECTABLE, with the exports following the selection rather than the filter: the filter narrows what you are looking at, the tick boxes decide what you are sending, and keeping them apart is what lets you filter to find three policies, tick them, clear the filter and still export three. Everything is selected after a read because the common case is 'document the tenant'; select all, select what is shown, invert and select none sit in a bar above the list, and a whole section can be ticked from its heading. With nothing selected the export buttons disappear \u2014 a button that would produce an empty document is worse than no button. Every export header now distinguishes A SELECTION from A FILTERED VIEW, because 'I picked these twelve' and 'a platform filter left twelve' are different claims and neither is 'this is the tenant'. v0.3 fixes the platform filter, which had nothing usable in it. Graph names the same platform three ways depending on which surface you ask \u2014 a settings-catalog policy says 'windows10', a device configuration implies 'Windows' through its type \u2014 and the first version passed whatever it found straight into the list, so a tenant with both offered TWO ENTRIES FOR ONE PLATFORM, each matching half the estate. A policy targeting two platforms arrived as the single opaque string 'windows10,macOS' and matched neither. Graph's literal 'none' appeared as though it were a platform. Everything normalises to one vocabulary now \u2014 Windows, macOS, iOS/iPadOS, Android, Linux \u2014 a policy carries a LIST because it really can target more than one, and a policy with no platform at all (a script, an assignment filter, a scope tag) is filterable as 'Not platform-specific' rather than being invisible in every filtered view. The filters are also switched off until a read, with a line saying why: the list is built from what the tenant returns and cannot exist before then, and an empty control with no explanation is exactly how this looked broken. v0.4 makes the list FIXED: all five platforms every time, plus the not-platform-specific bucket, each carrying its count. Building it from what the read returned sounded tidier and was worse in practice \u2014 the options changed shape from tenant to tenant, a single-platform estate got a control with one entry and nothing to do, and an admin who believes they have no Linux had no way to confirm it. A zero is an answer that an absent entry cannot give. The counts deliberately do not sum to the total, because a policy targeting two platforms is counted under both" },
  toolBackup: { t: 4, v: "0.3", note: "v0.3 (build 10426, R15 after Ugur Koc's TenuVault TUI, MIT) WIDENS AND HARDENS THE ARCHIVE. Nineteen export-only areas join the interop five — intents, Autopilot, enrolment configurations, the three update-profile kinds, macOS custom-attribute scripts, assignment filters, scope tags, device categories, terms and conditions, notification templates, app configuration both kinds, app protection for iOS/Android/Windows, WIP, app categories — each under its own read scope, consented as the union of what is CHOSEN, so picking only the five asks exactly what v0.1 asked. EVERY FILE CARRIES A SHA-256 in the manifest and THE MANIFEST GOES INTO THE ZIP LAST, TenuVault's atomicity rule kept exactly: its presence marks the backup complete. VERIFY RUNS OFFLINE in the tab — no sign-in — naming modified, missing and untracked files; an archive without a manifest is called a backup that never finished; a pre-checksum archive is UNVERIFIABLE, never quietly passed. ASSIGNMENTS EXPORT AND IMPORT (Mihai's amendment to the R15 card, which had said read-only): assignments.json carries every assigned policy's targets serialised through T11's cleaner — one implementation of what an assignment is — and imports back under three hard rules: SAME TENANT ONLY (the archive carries the tenant id, a mismatch is refused with no override, an identity-less older archive gates on typing the domain), ALL-OR-NOTHING PER POLICY when an archived list names a since-deleted group (writing the list without it changes its meaning; a dropped exclusion widens), and T11'S WRITE DISCIPLINE WHOLE — same policy ids never a namesake, four surfaces under the held write scope, dry run, fresh-read drift skip, read-back verify, sequential, never retried, the write scope asked at the click. Import is refused outright for a tampered-or-damaged archive. groups.json resolves every targeted group to its name AT BACKUP TIME, for the audit question the directory can no longer answer. Original v0.1/0.2 note: BETA \u2014 v0.2 (R09, after Ugur Koc's Restore Intune Configuration + two TenuVault rules): THE OTHER DIRECTION. Load an archive \u2014 this tool's or the original script's, the folder layout is shared and load-bearing \u2014 pick objects, dry-run, apply. CREATE ONLY: nothing of yours is patched or deleted; the one exception is TenuVault's, kept exactly \u2014 an ADMX template THIS restore half-created and failed to finish is rolled back, its own mess and nothing else. Every object arrives under an editable [Restored] prefix and UNASSIGNED (shipped narrower than the card's off-by-default assignment toggle, on purpose: a source-tenant group GUID either does not exist in the target or names a different group). THE COLLISION STOP: a name that exists in the tenant skips that object \u2014 checked at dry run AND re-checked fresh immediately before each create, because the tenant can change in between; overwrite is DELIBERATELY NOT OFFERED (a departure from the card's first draft, said on the card \u2014 overwrite is a patch wearing a restore's clothes). The dry run lists every operation INCLUDING the ADMX definition-value child writes the original's -WhatIf never shows; settings-catalog settings ride the create inline; compliance policies carry their scheduledActionsForRule with source-tenant ids stripped; ADMX definition binds go to Microsoft's own definition ids, which are the same in every tenant. Non-restorable files (script without a body) cannot be selected and say why. Created is the tenant's word: every create is verified by read-back. Scopes at the apply click: the config write for four areas, the scripts write for platform scripts \u2014 both already declared. \u2014 the configuration surface read out and written to one .zip in the browser: device configuration profiles, settings catalog policies WITH their settings, compliance policies with their scheduled actions, administrative templates with their definition values, and platform scripts (Windows PowerShell and macOS shell) with their bodies. After Ugur Koc's backup-intune-configuration.ps1, and THE FOLDER LAYOUT AND FILE NAMES ARE HIS EXACTLY \u2014 an archive written here restores with his script, one written by his script reads back here. That interoperability is worth more than any tidier scheme. Reads only, and one scope: DeviceManagementConfiguration.Read.All. THE N+1 IS THE WHOLE COST: three of the five areas need a second request per object because the list endpoint returns a count or a null where the content should be, and the original does those in series with a 100ms sleep, which on 400 policies is over a minute of a browser doing nothing. Bounded concurrency here, with errors captured per item. AN OBJECT WHOSE DETAIL COULD NOT BE READ IS LEFT OUT OF THE ARCHIVE rather than written without it \u2014 a settings-catalog policy restored with the right name and no settings is worse than one visibly missing. The manifest keeps the original's four fields so the shape stays familiar, and adds what it lacks: an index of every file with its type, Graph surface and whether it is restorable; the tenant it came from, because the question six months later is 'is this the right tenant'; and an explicit record of what the archive CANNOT restore. Two of those are stated up front rather than discovered at restore: Graph never returns secret values, so encrypted OMA-URI settings, passwords and certificates are references that must be re-entered; and a script saved without its body cannot be put back, which the original's -SkipScriptContent switch produces silently. An area that could not be read at all marks the whole archive PARTIAL. After Ugur Koc's Backup Intune Configuration (MIT)" },
  toolAudit: { t: 3, v: "0.2", note: "BETA \u2014 who changed Intune, what moved, and when. v0.2 (build 10413, Option A of a two-option mockup round) rebuilds the SCREEN without touching what is read: FOUR STAT CARDS answer the first questions before any scrolling \u2014 total changes, most active admin, most active area, failure rate, each computed from the rows currently shown so the filters move them too, and null on an empty window rather than a placeholder; A TIMELINE with an operation dot and badge per event \u2014 created, deleted, updated, assigned, action \u2014 classified from Graph's own activityOperationType with the activity text as fallback, coloured from the house tokens so the dark theme needs no second rule set; CLICK A CARD AND THE DETAIL UNFOLDS IN PLACE \u2014 actor with IP and kind, result and severity, correlation id, resource ids, and the full field-level diff \u2014 with the open set keyed on EVENT IDS so a filter change redraws the list and keeps your cards open; A RANGE PICKER with the presets plus a CUSTOM day range, where the from is clamped to the 30-day retention floor AND THE CLAMP IS REPORTED \u2014 a range that quietly shrank would read as a quiet tenant \u2014 and a picked day means the whole day, to 23:59:59Z, because half-open ranges off a date picker are how 'up to Tuesday' silently excludes Tuesday; and A FILTER DIALOG holding the same controls with the same wildcard semantics, plus operation type and an actor dropdown \u2014 category, operation and actor lists all built from what the tenant actually emitted, the rule the tool has had since 10323. Everything is DOM in this tab; the dialog and popover are divs, not portals, and filters still run over rows already read. One tool over deviceManagement/auditEvents with two views, because both are one Graph read and two tiles would be two copies of the same paging, throttling and export. POLICY CHANGES after Ugur Koc's check-policy-changes.ps1: configuration activity with a severity read off the activity type \u2014 a delete or a failure outranks a create, a create outranks an assignment change. ALL EVENTS after his get-intune-audit-logs.ps1: every category, filtered by actor, activity, category and result, with wildcards honoured as wildcards rather than quietly treated as substrings. THE DIFF IS ENCA'S T16, PORTED, and it is the reason to use this over the portal: modifiedProperties values arrive as JSON strings, sometimes double-encoded, sometimes wrapped in a one-element array, and the useful answer is a field-level list of what moved \u2014 assignment lists compared as sets so you see added and removed rather than two blobs. Both originals print one before/after pair and stop, which for a settings-catalog policy means several kilobytes of JSON in a CSV cell. THREE UPSTREAM BUGS FIXED: the two scripts window the same endpoint two ways and a bare date means midnight, so 'the last day' was anything from a minute to 24 hours \u2014 full ISO-8601 UTC in both; the fixed category list contains values Graph never emits, so choosing one returned nothing and read as a quiet tenant \u2014 categories come from the tenant's own data; and the policy view is not capped at five rows after paging everything. Every resource on a record is inspected rather than the first, because an assignment change puts the policy in slot 0 and the group in slot 1. Windows stop at 30 days because that is Intune's audit retention, and the screen says so rather than offering a 90-day option that returns a confidently empty answer. Reads only. After Ugur Koc's two monitoring scripts (MIT) and ENCA's T16" },
  toolRoles: { t: 7, v: "0.2", note: "BETA \u2014 v0.2 (build 10392): RENAMED to Intune RBAC \u2014 the tool is the RBAC surface, not one question about it. Role assignments becomes its FIRST SECTION, and the section seg is where scope tags, custom-role review and whatever else belongs to Intune RBAC will land \u2014 each as a section here rather than a new tile. One tool per surface, sections per question. Everything below is the Role assignments section, unchanged. \u2014 who can change Intune, on one page. Every role definition, built-in and custom, each assignment under it, the users and groups in it resolved to names, its scope tags and what it is scoped over. After Ugur Koc's get-intune-role-assignments.ps1 (MIT), whose question this is; the portal makes you click into one role at a time, which is why nobody audits this. NAMES ARE RESOLVED IN ONE CALL RATHER THAN TWO PER MEMBER: the original probes /users/{id} and then /groups/{id} for each principal, which on a surface where a hundred members is ordinary is up to two hundred round trips; directoryObjects/getByIds answers a thousand at once AND says what type each object is, so there is nothing to probe. TWO THINGS THE ORIGINAL DOES NOT READ, both of which change the answer: scope tags, which are how a tenant gives a regional helpdesk a role over only its own devices and whose absence overstates every scoped assignment; and the scope itself, read from scopeType and resourceScopes together, so that 'all devices' and 'Graph did not say' are different answers here \u2014 the original falls back to the literal string All when resourceScopes is empty, which is right often enough to be dangerous. EMPTY ROLES ARE A TOGGLE, not a hidden row: a role with nobody in it is one membership change from being a live grant, and switching it on re-renders what was already read rather than asking the tenant again. CONCENTRATIONS ARE OBSERVATIONS AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO SCORE \u2014 an assignment scoped to all devices, a broad built-in role with a lot of members at a threshold the screen states because it is arbitrary, a role whose members are all one group where whoever can change that group can grant the role without holding it. And the sentence neither the script nor the portal says, carried on the screen and in all three exports: THIS IS INTUNE RBAC ONLY. Global Administrator and Intune Administrator are Entra directory roles, they grant full access to Intune, and they do not appear in deviceManagement/roleAssignments \u2014 an access review that takes this for the whole list is missing the accounts with the most access. An assignment whose role definition could not be resolved is kept under a role that says so rather than dropped, because dropping it takes a live grant off the review. Exports Markdown, CSV one row per member, and a standalone HTML report. After Ugur Koc's Get Intune Role Assignments (MIT)" },
  toolDevice: { t: 6, v: "0.1", note: "BETA \u2014 R04's assignment graph walked backwards from one device. Find a machine by name, serial, Intune device id or Entra device id \u2014 every device has two GUIDs and which one is on the ticket is a coin toss, so both are tried \u2014 and see which of its own Entra groups, which of its primary user's groups, or which tenant-wide target put each policy there. THE ENDPOINT LIST IS NOT A SECOND COPY: T06 builds a match set and hands it to T02's own source table, because the first time a surface was added to one and not the other this tool would have started reporting a device as unaffected by a workload it never looked at, with nothing on screen to say so. TENANT-WIDE TARGETS ARE ON BY DEFAULT, which is the real difference from T02 rather than a preference \u2014 for a group, All Devices is the same answer every time and says nothing about the group you asked about; for a device it IS the answer, and hiding it would hide policy the machine is definitely getting. All Users lands on the person, so a device with no primary user does not receive it, and the row says that rather than being dropped. INTENDED AND ACTUAL ARE TWO COLUMNS and the gap between them is the point: everything from an assignment is what Intune intends, what the machine has is whatever it collected at its last check-in, so the check-in time is the first thing on the page and the report states that a policy assigned minutes ago has not applied. deviceConfigurationStates and deviceCompliancePolicyStates supply the device's own verdict where they answer; where they 403, come back empty, or cannot be tied to a policy with certainty \u2014 two policies sharing a name is enough \u2014 the state is unknown, never fine by omission. A FILTERED ASSIGNMENT IS AN OPEN QUESTION: the rule is evaluated by the service against inventory a browser cannot see and Graph has no call that answers it, so those rows say may reach it and name the filter. An exclusion beats an inclusion, except where the two arrive through different kinds of group \u2014 device against user \u2014 where whether it applies depends on how the policy is targeted and the assignment does not say; that is called a conflict rather than guessed. Adds one scope, Device.Read.All, because the Entra device object is not the Intune enrolment record. Exports Markdown, CSV and a standalone HTML report" },
  toolGroupUse: { t: 2, v: "0.4", note: "BETA — v0.4 (R19, after Alper Atar's IntuneShade, MIT): the sweep gains a second face — the ASSIGNMENT MATRIX, groups down the side, surfaces across the top, rendered from the sweep's one read with a Table/Matrix switch that asks the tenant nothing. A cell is a claim and the grid does not overstate it: includes are the count, exclusions their own red −n per surface (never folded in — 'does not reach you' drawn as coverage is the overstatement a matrix invites), dangling references wear the deleted flag instead of counting, and the EMPTY-GROUP CHECK is opt-in and absent-not-empty: one $top=1 transitive-members probe per group batched twenty a trip (T09's technique) behind a button that names its cost; a failed probe is 'reach?', unknown, never empty. A group click lands in single-group mode through the tile's own pipeline. — the first TUNO tool that reads a tenant. Name an Entra group and get everything Intune sends to its members across nine surfaces and twenty Graph endpoints: configuration profiles (device, settings catalog, ADMX), compliance, scripts and remediations (PowerShell, macOS shell, remediation), application assignments, app protection, app configuration, enrolment restrictions, Autopilot and Windows update profiles. Two parents, and the merge is the point. ENCA's Group Analyzer (T19) supplies the machinery — the source descriptor with its own scopes and runner, the flat hit shape, the rule that a failing source is reported and never fatal, and ancestors rather than just the group itself. Ugur Koc's Get Group Assignments supplies the Intune surface map and two things ENCA's version does not do: EXCLUSIONS ARE READ AS ASSIGNMENTS, because 'this policy does not reach you' is the same question as 'this policy reaches you' and a report showing only inclusions is wrong half the time; and TENANT-WIDE TARGETS ARE OFFERED as a toggle, because All Users and All Devices land on the group's members too and the effective surface is larger than the group's own assignments. And one thing neither has: the script's own notes say nested group inheritance is not evaluated, so an inherited assignment is included here and says which parent it came through. A surface that could not be read is never reported as empty — it is reported as unknown, with the permission and the Intune RBAC role it would need, because those are different things. Intune only, deliberately: where a group is used in Conditional Access, licensing, directory roles or access packages needs scopes TUNO has no reason to hold, and the tool points at ENCA rather than half-reading them. Exports Markdown, CSV and a standalone HTML report that opens for someone with no access to the tenant. v0.2 adds ENCA's TENANT SWEEP, with the Conditional Access scope replaced by its Intune equivalent. A sweep reads each surface ONCE and matches every group against it, so the cost barely grows with the group count — 300 groups is still twenty reads. 'Only groups Intune assigns to' takes the ids off the assignments as they are read, so there is no /groups enumeration at all; it is the fastest scope and it CANNOT find unused groups, which the screen says where the choice is made rather than leaving an empty list to be misread as a clean bill of health. The counted scopes enumerate and can. A name filter narrows the scan server-side where Graph supports the shape, falling back to listing and filtering locally — and the local check is always the final word, because $search matches tokens rather than substrings and returns more than it should. Group nesting is off by default and batched twenty at a time when on; with it off a group that only receives policy through a parent reads as zero, and the report says so rather than letting it pass as unused. An id an assignment names that the directory no longer has is kept and flagged as a DANGLING REFERENCE: that assignment targets nobody and nothing in the portal will tell you. After Ugur Koc's Get Group Assignments (MIT) and ENCA's T19" },
};
